Computational Development and the Emergence of Reasoning

A Formal Theory of Developmental Trajectories and Their Effect on the Geometry of Future Learning

A Theoretical Dissertation Proposal — Revised Version

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

---

Abstract

This dissertation proposes a formal framework for the study of cognitive development in artificial systems. The central claim is that the relevant scientific question is not whether a fixed architecture can attain high performance, but whether a developmental trajectory—characterized by structured intrinsic activity, endogenous plasticity, and transient scaffolds—transforms the geometry of future learning of an agent. We introduce a constrained Developmental Transfer Operator acting on a cost functional, a decomposition of the developmental distance into access and efficiency components, an extension to a Developmental Task Geometry distinguishing learning, representing, and generating, and a falsifiable experimental protocol with strict budget equalization. The revised framework adds three structural refinements: a regularity condition on the transfer operator rendering it quantitatively estimable; a constitutive distinction between Intrinsic Structured Activity and self-supervised pre-training grounded in non-task-derivation; and a predicted ordering of geometric changes that transforms the framework's central hypotheses from existential to ordinal claims. No empirical results are reported. The contribution is theoretical: a formal apparatus, a set of constrained hypotheses, and a set of protocols designed to render those hypotheses testable.

Keywords: computational development, intrinsic structured activity, endogenous plasticity, NeuroAI, transfer operator, developmental robotics, falsifiability.

