Chapter 2 — Formal Framework: Translation Chain

2.1 Definition of the Chain

Definition 2.1 (Translation chain). A translation chain is a sextuple:

\boxed{\;(B_i,\, C_i,\, I_i,\, A_i,\, T_i,\, F_i)\;}

where B_i is an observed biological phenomenon, C_i an identified biological constraint, I_i a candidate computational invariant, A_i a possible artificial implementation, T_i an experimental test, and F_i a falsification criterion.

2.2 Validity Conditions

Proposition 2.2. A translation chain is valid if and only if:

1. I_i is defined without reference to B_i (semantic independence);
2. A_i is implementable without access to B_i (technical independence);
3. F_i specifies an observation that would render I_i false (falsifiability).

Justification. Without (1), the invariant is a metaphorical restatement. Without (2), the implementation depends on a non-transposable biological observation. Without (3), the chain is unfalsifiable. \square

2.3 Application to Developmental Levels

Level B_i C_i I_i F_i (abridged)
0 Genome, gradients Compression, resources Self-organization under constraints If A = B = C, development unnecessary
1 Prolonged neurogenesis Proliferation duration Duration as architectural parameter If fixed architecture equivalent, unnecessary
2 Migration, differentiation Separated lineages Two populations, progressive integration If late recruitment without effect, unnecessary
3 Spontaneous activity, subplate Early self-organization ISA If C ≈ D, structure unnecessary

