Chapter 3 — Developmental Constraints (Levels 0–3)

3.1 Level 0 — Initial Constraints

Biological fact 3.1. The human genome (approximately 2 \times 10^4 genes) cannot specify approximately 10^{11} neurons or 10^{14} synapses. It encodes generative rules: morphogen gradients, transcriptional networks, resource constraints.

Candidate invariant I_0. A system can develop a functional organization from a small set of generative rules, without explicit specification of the final architecture.

3.2 Levels 1–2 — Growth and Connectivity

Biological fact 3.2. Human cortical neurogenesis lasts approximately 4 months (versus approximately 1 week in mice). The outer subventricular zone (oSVZ) is a site of specifically human proliferation.

Biological fact 3.3. Migration is radial (excitatory) and tangential (inhibitory). In humans, postnatal migration persists for several months.

Candidate invariant I_1. Proliferation duration is an architectural parameter.

Candidate invariant I_2. Late integration of regulatory populations is a mechanism of targeted plasticity.

3.3 Level 3 — Intrinsic Structured Activity

Biological fact 3.4. Structured firing sequences—some rigid, others flexible—emerge in the spontaneous activity of human and murine brain organoids, as well as in ex vivo neonatal murine cortical slices, but not in dissociated primary cortical cultures. These sequences are constrained by a preconfigured architecture established during neurodevelopment, rather than by experience. The critical variable is the organized three-dimensional tissue architecture, not the statistical properties of any external input.

Definition 3.5 (ISA). Intrinsic Structured Activity is defined as:

\boxed{\;\text{ISA} \;=\; \underbrace{\text{endogenous activity}}_{\text{no external input}} \;+\; \underbrace{\text{spatiotemporal structure}}_{\text{preconfigured sequences}} \;+\; \underbrace{\text{developmental coupling}}_{\text{circuit maturation}} \;+\; \underbrace{\text{non-task-derivation}}_{\text{independent of downstream } \mu}\;}

Remark 3.6. ISA \neq structured noise. Structured noise can only bias weights. Preconfigured endogenous activity can instantiate a temporal architecture—sequences that serve as prototypes for future network dynamics.

Remark 3.7. The fourth component is constitutive, not optional. Self-supervised pre-training learns structure from data; ISA instantiates structure from architecture. The organoid evidence demonstrates that structured sequences emerge in three-dimensional tissue but not in dissociated cultures—indicating that the generative source is architectural, not statistical. If the sequences in a candidate implementation are task-derived, the implementation is a curriculum, not ISA.

Hypothesis H₃. ISA coupled with plasticity and a transient scaffold produces a persistent functional organization.

Hypothesis H₃₊. ISA modifies not only performance, but the geometry of future learning costs (Chapter 4).

