Chapter 4 — Developmental Transfer Operator

4.1 Developmental State

Definition 4.1 (Developmental state).

X_t \;=\; (W_t,\, P_t,\, Q_t,\, M_t,\, E_t,\, C_t)

where W_t is connectivity, P_t plasticity, Q_t scaffolds, M_t memory, E_t resources, and C_t structural connectivity.

Definition 4.2 (Structural degrees of freedom).

\boxed{\;\Theta_t \;=\; (C_t,\, P_t,\, Q_t,\, \mathcal{R}_t^{\text{learn}})\;}

where \mathcal{R}_t^{\text{learn}} denotes available learning rules. \Theta_t represents the metaplastic and structural properties of the learner, as opposed to W_t, which represents its content.

Definition 4.3 (Dynamics).

X_{t+1} \;=\; F(X_t,\, I_t,\, O_t,\, A_t)

with endogenous plasticity:

P_{t+1} \;=\; f_P(X_t,\, I_t,\, O_t,\, A_t).

Regularity assumption 4.4. F and f_P are Lipschitz with constant L_F.

4.2 Learning Capacity

Definition 4.5 (Robust cost). For \epsilon, \delta > 0:

T_\tau(X_t; \epsilon, \delta) \;=\; \min\!\left\{n : \mathbb{P}\!\left[\mathrm{Perf}_\tau(\theta_n) \geq 1 - \epsilon\right] \geq 1 - \delta\right\}

Definition 4.6 (Budget).

B \;=\; (B_{\text{data}},\, B_{\text{compute}},\, B_{\text{mem}},\, B_{\text{energy}},\, B_{\text{params}})

Definition 4.7 (Learning capacity).

\boxed{\;\mathcal{A}_t(\tau; \epsilon, \delta, B) \;=\; T_\tau^{B}(X_t; \epsilon, \delta)\;}

Remark 4.8. \mathcal{A}_t : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}^+ \cup \{+\infty\} is a functional, not a set. It is the geometry of learning costs.

4.3 Non-Trivial Developmental Transfer Operator

Definition 4.9 (Learning space).

\mathcal{L}_t \;=\; \left\{\tau \in \mathcal{T} : \mathcal{A}_t(\tau) \leq T_{\max}\right\}

Proposition 4.10. \mathcal{L}_t is determined by \mathcal{A}_t. The converse is false.

Proof. \mathcal{L}_t = \mathcal{A}_t^{-1}([0, T_{\max}]). For the converse, take two functionals that coincide on [0, T_{\max}] but differ beyond. \square

Definition 4.11 (Developmental transfer operator).

\boxed{\;\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R},\,t \to t+\Delta} \;:\; (X_t,\, \mathcal{A}_t) \;\longmapsto\; (X_{t+\Delta},\, \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta})\;}

where \mathcal{R} denotes the full developmental trajectory \{X_s\}_{s=t}^{t+\Delta}.

Explicit form.

\boxed{\;\mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta} \;=\; \Psi_\Delta\!\left(\mathcal{A}_t,\, \mathcal{R}[t, t+\Delta]\right)\;}

where \Psi_\Delta is a functional depending explicitly on the trajectory.

Regularity condition 4.12 (Lipschitz in trajectory). The trajectory-dependent update \Psi_\Delta satisfies:

\|\Psi_\Delta(\mathcal{A}_t, \mathcal{R}_1) - \Psi_\Delta(\mathcal{A}_t, \mathcal{R}_2)\| \;\leq\; L_\Psi \cdot d_{\text{traj}}(\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2)

where d_{\text{traj}} is a metric on trajectories, e.g., d_{\text{traj}}(\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2) = \int_t^{t+\Delta} \|P_s^{(1)} - P_s^{(2)}\| + \|Q_s^{(1)} - Q_s^{(2)}\| + \|I_s^{(1)} - I_s^{(2)}\| \, ds.

Volterra expansion 4.13. \Psi_\Delta admits a Volterra expansion in the trajectory:

\mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta}(\tau) = \mathcal{A}_t(\tau) + \int_t^{t+\Delta} K_1(s) \cdot \delta P_s \, ds + \int_t^{t+\Delta} \int_t^{t+\Delta} K_2(s,s') \cdot \delta Q_s \delta Q_{s'} \, ds \, ds' + \cdots

where \delta P_s = P_s - P_{\text{baseline}}, \delta Q_s = Q_s - Q_{\text{baseline}}, and K_1, K_2 are kernels. This renders the operator estimable: K_1 captures the linear effect of plasticity, K_2 the scaffold interaction, and higher-order kernels the nonlinear coupling.

Remark 4.14 (Non-triviality). The Lipschitz condition and Volterra expansion transform H_{3+} from an existential claim into a quantitative one: the magnitude of the developmental effect scales with the structural distance between trajectories, and the relative contribution of plasticity versus scaffold is measurable.

Proposition 4.15 (Composition).

\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R},\,t \to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2} \;=\; \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}',\,t+\Delta_1 \to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2} \circ \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R},\,t \to t+\Delta_1}

Proof. Immediate from the definition. \square

4.4 Developmental Distances

Definition 4.16 (Normalized distance).

\tilde{T}_\tau^{(i)} \;=\; \frac{\mathcal{A}_T^{(i)}(\tau)}{T_{\max}} \in [0,1]

with the convention \tilde{T} = 1 outside \mathcal{L}_A \cup \mathcal{L}_C.

\boxed{\;d_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) \;=\; \int_{\mathcal{T}} \left|\tilde{T}_\tau^{(A)} - \tilde{T}_\tau^{(C)}\right| d\mu(\tau)\;}

Proposition 4.17 (Decomposition).

d_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) \;\leq\; d_{\mathcal{L}}(A, C) + d_{\text{intra}}(A, C)

with:

d_{\mathcal{L}} = \int_{\mathcal{L}_A \triangle \mathcal{L}_C} d\mu, \qquad d_{\text{intra}} = \int_{\mathcal{L}_A \cap \mathcal{L}_C} \left|\tilde{T}^{(A)} - \tilde{T}^{(C)}\right| d\mu.

Proof. Partition of \mathcal{T} into \mathcal{L}_A \triangle \mathcal{L}_C and \mathcal{L}_A \cap \mathcal{L}_C, with the bound \tilde{T} \leq 1 on the symmetric difference. \square

Corollary 4.18. Two distinct developmental modes are detectable: (i) access to new tasks (d_{\mathcal{L}} > 0), and (ii) increased efficiency on the same tasks (d_{\text{intra}} > 0).

4.5 Development versus Curriculum

Definition 4.19 (Curriculum). A curriculum is an operator on the data distribution:

\mathcal{C} : \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{D}', \qquad \boxed{\;\dot{\Theta}_t = 0\;}

The learner is structurally fixed; only the data change.

Definition 4.20 (Development). Development is an operator that modifies the structural degrees of freedom of the learner:

\boxed{\;\mathcal{D} : \Theta_t \to \Theta_{t+\Delta}, \qquad \dot{\Theta}_t \neq 0\;}

Remark 4.21. The distinction does not rest on coupling within F, but on the nature of the modified object. A curriculum can modify W_t (content) without modifying \Theta_t (structure). Development modifies \Theta_t itself.

Proposition 4.22 (Operational criterion). A trajectory is developmental if and only if at least one component of \Theta_t—available connectivity C_t, plasticity P_t, scaffolds Q_t, learning rules \mathcal{R}_t^{\text{learn}}—evolves non-trivially.

4.6 Extension: Developmental Task Geometry

Definition 4.23 (Representable space).

\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}} \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \exists \phi_t, \exists D_\tau : \mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathrm{Perf}_\tau(D_\tau \circ \phi_t)\right] > \mathrm{Perf}_{\text{chance}}(\tau) + \eta.

Definition 4.24 (Syntactically generable space).

\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{syn}} \;=\; \mathrm{Closure}\!\left(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{prim}},\, \mathcal{O}_t\right)

where \mathcal{O}_t is the set of available composition operators.

Definition 4.25 (Valid generative space).

\boxed{\;\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}} \;=\; \left\{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{syn}} \;:\; V_t(\tau) \geq v_{\min}\right\}\;}

where V_t is a measure of task validity or executability.

Remark 4.26. Without the filter V_t, a generator of absurd compositions would produce an artificially elevated cardinality. The filter ensures that \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}} measures significant generative capacity, not raw syntactic productivity.

A priori validity condition 4.27. For each task family \mathcal{T}_f, V_t is a hand-designed predicate with three components:

V_t(\tau) \;=\; \mathrm{Synt}(\tau) \cdot \mathrm{Sem}(\tau) \cdot \mathrm{Exec}(\tau)

where \mathrm{Synt}(\tau) \in \{0,1\} is syntactic well-formedness defined by the family grammar, \mathrm{Sem}(\tau) \in [0,1] is semantic coherence defined by a fixed set of equivalence relations, and \mathrm{Exec}(\tau) \in [0,1] is executability defined by a simulator or environment oracle. This fixes V_t independently of the agent's developmental state, eliminating the regress that would arise if V_t were learned by a process dependent on \Theta_t.

Proposition 4.28 (Inclusions).

\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{syn}}, \qquad \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}}, \qquad \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{learn}} \subseteq \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}}.

Definition 4.29 (Developmental geometry).

\boxed{\;\mathcal{G}_t \;=\; \left(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}},\, \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{learn}},\, \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}},\, \mathcal{O}_t,\, \mathcal{A}_t\right)\;}

Definition 4.30 (Extended operator).

\mathcal{D}^{\mathcal{G}}_{\mathcal{R},\,t \to t+\Delta} \;=\; \left(\mathcal{D}^{\text{rep}},\, \mathcal{D}^{\text{learn}},\, \mathcal{D}^{\text{gen}},\, \mathcal{D}^{\mathcal{O}},\, \mathcal{D}^{\mathcal{A}}\right).

4.7 Predicted Ordering of Geometric Changes

Prediction 4.31 (Ordering). Under ISA-driven development, the geometric components evolve in the order:

\boxed{\;\mathcal{T}^{\text{rep}} \;\to\; \mathcal{T}^{\text{learn}} \;\to\; \mathcal{T}^{\text{gen}}\;}

Justification. ISA provides a preconfigured temporal architecture that first organizes representations (making previously unrepresentable tasks representable), then improves learning efficiency on now-representable tasks (via scaffold-mediated consolidation), and finally enables generation (composition of learned primitives into novel tasks). This ordering is testable: at early developmental times, \mathcal{T}^{\text{rep}} should expand first; \mathcal{T}^{\text{learn}} should follow; \mathcal{T}^{\text{gen}} should follow last. If the ordering is reversed, or if all three move simultaneously, the mechanism proposed is not the one operating.

Remark 4.32. The ordering prediction transforms the framework's central hypotheses from existential to ordinal claims. An existential claim ("there exist trajectories that differ") is weak; an ordinal claim ("the geometric components move in a specified order") is strong and specific. If the ordering is wrong, the mechanism is wrong.

4.8 Monotonicity: Definition and Hypothesis

Definition 4.33 (Monotone development on \tau).

\text{Monotone dev on } \tau \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta}(\tau) \leq \mathcal{A}_t(\tau).

Hypothesis H_{\text{mono}}.

\mu\!\left(\left\{\tau : \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta}(\tau) > \mathcal{A}_t(\tau)\right\}\right) \approx 0.

Remark 4.34. H_{\text{mono}} is an empirical hypothesis, not a mathematical consequence. A system may gain plasticity while temporarily becoming less efficient on a given task (interference, reorganization cost, representational shift). H_{\text{mono}} must be tested, not presumed.

4.9 Testable Hypotheses

Hypothesis H₃₊ (strong form). There exist \mathcal{R}_{\text{dev}}^{(1)} \neq \mathcal{R}_{\text{dev}}^{(2)} such that:

P_T^{(1)} \approx P_T^{(2)} \quad \text{and} \quad d_{\mathcal{A}}(A_1, A_2) > 0.

Hypothesis H₄₊ (weak form). There exist \mathcal{R}_{\text{dev}}^{(1)} \neq \mathcal{R}_{\text{dev}}^{(2)} such that:

\mathcal{A}_T^{(1)} \approx \mathcal{A}_T^{(2)} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen},(1)} \neq \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen},(2)}.

Hypothesis H₄₊ (strong form). There exist \mathcal{R}_{\text{dev}}^{(1)} \neq \mathcal{R}_{\text{dev}}^{(2)} such that:

\mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen},(1)} \supsetneq \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen},(2)}

with a measurable gap in composition depth, persistent after budget equalization.

Caveat 4.35. H₄₊ does not demonstrate the emergence of abstract reasoning. It demonstrates a difference in the generative space of problems. Reasoning would require additional properties (logical consistency, truth preservation under composition, systematic transferability).

4.10 Falsification Hierarchy

\boxed{\;H_3 \;\Rightarrow\; H_{3+} \;\Rightarrow\; H_{4+} \;\Rightarrow\; H_{\text{reasoning}}\;}

Hypothesis Statement Level
H_3 ISA produces a persistent organization Organization
H_{3+} This organization modifies future learning costs Learning capacity
H_{4+} It modifies the space of representable/generable problems Task geometry
H_{\text{reasoning}} This modification possesses the additional properties of abstract reasoning Cognition

