Chapter 5 — Experimental Protocol Design

This chapter specifies a protocol. No execution is reported.

5.1 Conditions

Definition 5.1 (Four conditions).

Agent ISA Scaffold Q Plasticity P(t) Maturation
A No No Constant No
B Yes (structured) No Decreasing Yes
C Yes (structured) Yes Decreasing Yes
D No (noise) Yes Decreasing Yes

Condition 5.2 (Spectral control of D). If S_{I_C}(\omega) is the power spectral density of ISA, then the noise of D is:

I_D(t) \;=\; \mathcal{F}^{-1}\!\left[\sqrt{S_{I_C}(\omega)} \cdot e^{i\phi_{\text{rand}}(\omega)}\right].

5.2 Budget Equalization

Principle 5.3. The budget vector B is identical across A, B, C, and D on all five components. Any developmental consumption is accounted for and compensated. If C consumes c_{\text{ISA}} FLOPs for ISA, then A receives c_{\text{ISA}} additional FLOPs of learning on the data.

Procedure 5.4 (Refined compensation protocol). If additional training on the post-developmental task set does not exhaust the compensation budget—because the control condition has converged—then:

1. Introduce random restarts of the control condition with different seeds.
2. Allocate the remaining compute to hyperparameter search over the control's learning rate, architecture width, and batch size.
3. Report the best control performance across all restarts and searches.

This gives the control condition the maximum possible advantage, making the developmental effect (if observed) conservative.

5.3 Metrics

Definition 5.5 (Trajectory metrics).

\mathcal{M} \;=\; \left(L(t),\, G,\, T_{1 \to 2},\, R,\, S_Q,\, \eta,\, D_{\text{repr}}\right)

where L(t) is learning speed, G compositional generalization, T_{1 \to 2} transfer cost, R developmental robustness, S_Q scaffold internalization, \eta energy efficiency, and D_{\text{repr}} a vector of structured representational diversity.

Definition 5.6 (Critical test).

\Delta L \;=\; \frac{dP_C}{dD} - \frac{dP_A}{dD}

measured after scaffold extinction and resource equalization.

5.4 Falsification Table

Observation Interpretation
C \approx A ISA without persistent effect
C \approx B Scaffold unnecessary
C \approx D Activity structure unnecessary
\Delta L > 0 then decaying Initialization effect, not developmental
\Delta L > 0 persistent, C > D Candidate developmental invariant

5.5 Statistical Protocol and Power Analysis

Procedure 5.7. N \geq 100 tasks, K \geq 10 repetitions, permutation test, threshold p < 0.01 after Bonferroni correction, effect size reported.

Power analysis 5.8. For a two-sample permutation test with N = 100 tasks, K = 10 repetitions, and Bonferroni correction across F = 5 families, the minimum detectable effect size (Cohen's d) at power 1 - \beta = 0.80 and \alpha = 0.01 is approximately d \approx 0.52. If the expected developmental effect is smaller—and the literature on similar interventions suggests effect sizes in the range d = 0.4–0.6—then either N or K must be increased. The expected effect size must be specified based on a priori reasoning or pilot data, and the required sample size computed accordingly.

Bias consideration 5.9. The estimator bias is \mathcal{O}(1/K). For K = 10, the bias is approximately 0.1—comparable to the expected effect. Either K must be increased, a bias-corrected estimator used, or the bias must be demonstrated to affect control and developmental conditions equally (orthogonality to the effect). The third option is most practical but requires justification.

