Appendix B — Proofs and Derivations

B.1 Proof of Proposition 2.2 (Validity Conditions)

Proposition. A translation chain is valid if and only if the three conditions of semantic independence, technical independence, and falsifiability hold.

Proof.

Necessity. Suppose one condition fails.

· If semantic independence fails, then I_i is expressible only by reference to B_i. Then I_i is not a computational invariant but a biological restatement. The chain cannot generate a test independent of biology.
· If technical independence fails, then A_i requires access to B_i to be implemented. Then any observed effect in A_i is confounded with the biological phenomenon itself.
· If falsifiability fails, then no observation F_i exists that would render I_i false. Then I_i is not a scientific claim.

Sufficiency. If all three conditions hold, then I_i is a computational claim, A_i an independent implementation, and F_i a test. The chain thereby produces a scientific prediction. \square

B.2 Proof of Proposition 4.10 (Determination of \mathcal{L}_t)

Proposition. \mathcal{L}_t is determined by \mathcal{A}_t, but the converse is false.

Proof.

Forward direction. By Definition 4.9, \mathcal{L}_t = \mathcal{A}_t^{-1}([0, T_{\max}]). Given \mathcal{A}_t and T_{\max}, \mathcal{L}_t is uniquely determined.

Converse. Construct two functionals \mathcal{A}^{(1)}, \mathcal{A}^{(2)} : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}^+ \cup \{+\infty\} such that:

\mathcal{A}^{(1)}(\tau) = \mathcal{A}^{(2)}(\tau) \quad \forall \tau \in \mathcal{A}^{(1)-1}([0, T_{\max}]),

but

\mathcal{A}^{(1)}(\tau^*) \neq \mathcal{A}^{(2)}(\tau^*) \quad \text{for some } \tau^* \text{ with } \mathcal{A}^{(1)}(\tau^*) > T_{\max}.

Then \mathcal{L}^{(1)} = \mathcal{L}^{(2)} but \mathcal{A}^{(1)} \neq \mathcal{A}^{(2)}. \square

B.3 Proof of Proposition 4.15 (Composition)

Proposition. \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}, t \to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2} = \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}', t+\Delta_1 \to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2} \circ \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}, t \to t+\Delta_1}.

Proof. Let \mathcal{R} = \{X_s\}_{s=t}^{t+\Delta_1} and \mathcal{R}' = \{X_s\}_{s=t+\Delta_1}^{t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}. By Definition 4.11:

\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}, t \to t+\Delta_1}(X_t, \mathcal{A}_t) = (X_{t+\Delta_1}, \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta_1}),

\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}', t+\Delta_1 \to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}(X_{t+\Delta_1}, \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta_1}) = (X_{t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}, \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}).

The composition yields:

(X_{t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}, \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}) = \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R} \cup \mathcal{R}', t \to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2}(X_t, \mathcal{A}_t).

Since \mathcal{R} \cup \mathcal{R}' is the trajectory from t to t+\Delta_1+\Delta_2, the identity holds. \square

B.4 Proof of Proposition 4.17 (Distance Decomposition)

Proposition. d_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) \leq d_{\mathcal{L}}(A, C) + d_{\text{intra}}(A, C).

Proof. Partition \mathcal{T} into three disjoint sets:

\mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{L}_A \setminus \mathcal{L}_C) \cup (\mathcal{L}_C \setminus \mathcal{L}_A) \cup (\mathcal{L}_A \cap \mathcal{L}_C).

Then:

d_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) = \int_{\mathcal{L}_A \triangle \mathcal{L}_C} |\tilde{T}^{(A)} - \tilde{T}^{(C)}| d\mu + \int_{\mathcal{L}_A \cap \mathcal{L}_C} |\tilde{T}^{(A)} - \tilde{T}^{(C)}| d\mu.

On the symmetric difference, \tilde{T}^{(i)} \in [0, 1] by Definition 4.16, so |\tilde{T}^{(A)} - \tilde{T}^{(C)}| \leq 1. Therefore:

\int_{\mathcal{L}_A \triangle \mathcal{L}_C} |\tilde{T}^{(A)} - \tilde{T}^{(C)}| d\mu \leq \int_{\mathcal{L}_A \triangle \mathcal{L}_C} d\mu = d_{\mathcal{L}}(A, C).

The second integral is d_{\text{intra}}(A, C) by definition. \square

B.5 Consistency of the Lipschitz Condition 4.12 with the Volterra Expansion 4.13

Proposition B.5. If the Volterra kernels satisfy \int_t^{t+\Delta} \|K_1(s)\| ds + \int_t^{t+\Delta}\int_t^{t+\Delta} \|K_2(s,s')\| ds\,ds' + \cdots \leq L_\Psi, then \Psi_\Delta satisfies the Lipschitz condition 4.12 with respect to d_{\text{traj}}.

Proof. Let \mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2 be two trajectories with perturbations \delta P^{(1)}, \delta P^{(2)}, etc. Then:

\Psi_\Delta(\mathcal{A}_t, \mathcal{R}_1) - \Psi_\Delta(\mathcal{A}_t, \mathcal{R}_2) = \int_t^{t+\Delta} K_1(s)(\delta P_s^{(1)} - \delta P_s^{(2)}) ds + \int_t^{t+\Delta}\int_t^{t+\Delta} K_2(s,s')(\delta Q_s^{(1)}\delta Q_{s'}^{(1)} - \delta Q_s^{(2)}\delta Q_{s'}^{(2)}) ds\,ds' + \cdots

Bounding each term by the kernel norm and the trajectory distance:

\|\Psi_\Delta(\mathcal{A}_t, \mathcal{R}_1) - \Psi_\Delta(\mathcal{A}_t, \mathcal{R}_2)\| \leq \left( \int \|K_1\| + \int\int \|K_2\| + \cdots \right) \cdot d_{\text{traj}}(\mathcal{R}_1, \mathcal{R}_2).

Setting L_\Psi equal to the parenthetical quantity yields the claim. \square

Remark B.6. The proposition establishes that the Lipschitz condition is not an additional assumption but a consequence of Volterra kernel summability. Conversely, if the kernel norms diverge, the Lipschitz condition fails and the Volterra expansion is not a valid representation of \Psi_\Delta.

B.6 Derivation of the Spectral Control (Condition 5.2)

Let I_C(t) be the ISA signal with power spectral density S_{I_C}(\omega). We construct I_D(t) such that:

S_{I_D}(\omega) = S_{I_C}(\omega) \quad \forall \omega,

but the phase structure of I_D is random. Define:

\tilde{I}_D(\omega) = \sqrt{S_{I_C}(\omega)} \cdot e^{i \phi_{\text{rand}}(\omega)},

where \phi_{\text{rand}}(\omega) \sim \mathrm{Uniform}([0, 2\pi]) independently for each \omega. Then:

I_D(t) = \mathcal{F}^{-1}[\tilde{I}_D](t) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} \int_{\mathbb{R}} \tilde{I}_D(\omega) e^{i \omega t} d\omega.

By construction, S_{I_D} = |\tilde{I}_D|^2 = S_{I_C}. The cross-spectral density between I_D and any reference signal is destroyed by the random phase. Thus D preserves the marginal spectrum but not the temporal structure. \square

B.7 Non-Task-Derivation as a Necessary Condition for ISA

Proposition B.7. Let \mathcal{S} = \{s_k\}_{k=1}^K be the sequence bank of an ISA generator. If \mathcal{S} is derived from the task distribution \mu—i.e., if there exists a measurable map \phi : \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{S} such that s_k = \phi(\tau_k) for tasks \tau_k \sim \mu—then the implementation is a curriculum, not ISA.

Proof. By Definition 4.19, a curriculum is an operator on the data distribution that leaves \Theta_t invariant. If \mathcal{S} is derived from \mu, then the sequences carry information about \mu and can be reconstructed from samples of \mu. The developmental effect is then attributable to the information content of \mathcal{S} about \mu, not to the preconfigured architecture. The learner's structural degrees of freedom \Theta_t are modified only through the statistical structure of \mathcal{S}, which is a property of the data distribution, not of the architecture. Hence \dot{\Theta}_t = 0 in the sense of Definition 4.19, and the trajectory is a curriculum. \square

Corollary B.8. An ISA implementation satisfies the non-task-derivation component (Definition 3.5) if and only if the sequence bank \mathcal{S} is independent of \mu in the sense of statistical independence: P(\mathcal{S} \mid \mu) = P(\mathcal{S}).

Remark B.9. The independence condition is stronger than non-derivation from samples. It requires that the distribution of the sequence bank not be conditionally dependent on the task distribution. This is testable: for two different task distributions \mu_1, \mu_2, the ISA generator must produce statistically indistinguishable sequence banks.

