Appendix C — Estimators and Statistical Properties

C.1 Estimator of \mathcal{A}_t

Definition C.1. For a task \tau and a state X_t, the empirical estimator \hat{T}_\tau(X_t; \epsilon, \delta) is:

\hat{T}_\tau(X_t; \epsilon, \delta) = \min \left\{ n : \hat{p}_n(\tau) \geq 1 - \epsilon \right\},

where \hat{p}_n(\tau) is the empirical success probability after n updates, estimated over K independent runs.

Proposition C.2 (Consistency). Under the assumption that the learning curve \mathcal{C}_\tau(n; X_t) is continuous and strictly increasing in n, \hat{T}_\tau \to T_\tau almost surely as K \to \infty.

Proof. By the strong law of large numbers, \hat{p}_n(\tau) \to \mathbb{P}[\mathrm{Perf}_\tau(\theta_n) \geq 1 - \epsilon] almost surely for each n. Since \mathcal{C}_\tau is continuous and strictly increasing, the smallest n with \mathcal{C}_\tau(n) \geq 1 - \epsilon is the unique limit of the empirical minimizers. \square

C.2 Estimator of d_{\mathcal{A}}

Definition C.3. For a finite set \mathcal{T}_N = \{\tau_1, \dots, \tau_N\} and K repetitions:

\hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left| \hat{\tilde{T}}_{\tau_i}^{(A)} - \hat{\tilde{T}}_{\tau_i}^{(C)} \right|.

Proposition C.4 (Bias). Under regularity of the learning curves, the bias satisfies:

\mathbb{E}[\hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}] - d_{\mathcal{A}} = \mathcal{O}(1/K).

Proof. By Taylor expansion of \hat{T}_\tau around T_\tau, the error is of order K^{-1/2} per task. Averaging over N tasks reduces the variance by N^{-1} but leaves the bias of order K^{-1/2}. For a Lipschitz transformation |\cdot|, the bias propagates linearly, giving \mathcal{O}(K^{-1/2}) per term. The asserted \mathcal{O}(1/K) holds under the additional assumption that the estimator is unbiased to second order, which is satisfied when the learning curve is locally linear near the threshold. \square

C.3 Bias Orthogonality Condition

Condition C.5. The estimator bias is orthogonal to the developmental effect if:

\mathbb{E}[\hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C)] - d_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) = \mathbb{E}[\hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}(A', C')] - d_{\mathcal{A}}(A', C')

for all pairs of conditions (A, C) and (A', C') under comparison.

Remark C.6. If Condition C.5 holds, the bias cancels in the difference \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}(C, A) - \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}(C, D) and does not confound the critical test \Delta L. If it does not hold, the bias must be estimated and subtracted, which requires additional assumptions.

C.4 Permutation Test

Procedure C.7.

1. Compute \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) from the observed data.
2. Generate M permuted datasets by randomly swapping the agent labels on pairs (\hat{T}_\tau^{(A)}, \hat{T}_\tau^{(C)}).
3. Compute \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}^{(m)} for each permutation m = 1, \dots, M.
4. Define the p-value:

p = \frac{1 + \#\{m : \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}^{(m)} \geq \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}\}}{1 + M}.

Justification. Under the null hypothesis d_{\mathcal{A}}(A, C) = 0, the labels are exchangeable. The permutation distribution is therefore a valid approximation of the null distribution of \hat{d}_{\mathcal{A}}. \square

C.5 Power Analysis

Definition C.8. For a two-sample test with N tasks per condition, K repetitions per task, and effect size d = d_{\mathcal{A}} / \sigma_{\mathcal{A}}, the power 1 - \beta satisfies:

1 - \beta = \Phi\left( z_{\alpha/2} - d \sqrt{N_{\text{eff}}} \right),

where N_{\text{eff}} = N \cdot K is the effective sample size and \Phi is the standard normal CDF.

Proposition C.9 (Minimum detectable effect). For N = 100, K = 10, F = 5 families, \alpha = 0.01 after Bonferroni correction, and target power 1 - \beta = 0.80, the minimum detectable effect size is approximately:

d_{\min} \approx z_{\alpha/2F} \cdot \sqrt{\frac{2}{N \cdot K}} + z_{1-\beta} \cdot \sqrt{\frac{2}{N \cdot K}} \approx 0.52.

Implication C.10. If the expected developmental effect is smaller than d_{\min}, the protocol must be modified: either N increased, K increased, F reduced, or the threshold relaxed. The expected effect size must be specified a priori based on pilot data or theoretical reasoning.

C.6 Multiple Comparison Correction

Procedure C.11. For F families of tasks (compositional, transfer, out-of-distribution, etc.), apply Bonferroni correction:

\alpha_{\text{corrected}} = \frac{\alpha}{F}.

Reject the null hypothesis for family f if p_f < \alpha_{\text{corrected}}.

Alternative C.12. For correlated families, apply the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure to control the false discovery rate at level q:

\text{Reject } H_{(i)} \text{ if } p_{(i)} \leq \frac{i}{F} q,

where p_{(1)} \leq \dots \leq p_{(F)} are the ordered p-values.

C.7 Ordering Test

Procedure C.13 (Test of Prediction 4.31). To test the predicted ordering \mathcal{T}^{\text{rep}} \to \mathcal{T}^{\text{learn}} \to \mathcal{T}^{\text{gen}}:

1. Estimate \hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}}), \hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{learn}}), \hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}) at a sequence of developmental times t_1 < t_2 < \dots < t_m.
2. For each pair (i, j) of geometric components, compute the time t_{ij}^* at which component j first exceeds its baseline.
3. The ordering prediction is confirmed if t_{\text{rep}}^* < t_{\text{learn}}^* < t_{\text{gen}}^* with statistical significance under a one-sided test.

Proposition C.14. Under the null hypothesis of simultaneous geometric change, the probability of observing the predicted strict ordering by chance is 1/3! = 1/6. With three independent replications, the probability of three confirmations by chance is 1/216 \approx 0.005 < 0.01, satisfying the Bonferroni-corrected threshold.

