Appendix D — Specification of the ISA Generator

D.1 General Architecture

Definition D.1 (ISA generator). The ISA generator is a stochastic process:

G_\theta : \mathcal{Z} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^N, \quad (z, t) \mapsto I(t),

where z \sim p_z is a latent variable and I(t) \in \mathbb{R}^N is the intrinsic activity at time t.

D.2 Requirements

Requirement D.2 (Temporal structure). The autocorrelation function R_I(\Delta t) = \mathbb{E}[I(t) I(t+\Delta t)] must decay on a timescale \tau_I > 0 significantly larger than the discretization step.

Requirement D.3 (Spatial structure). The instantaneous covariance C_I(t) = \mathbb{E}[I(t) I(t)^\top] must exhibit non-trivial off-diagonal structure, i.e., there exist indices i \neq j with |C_I(t)_{ij}| > \epsilon_I for some \epsilon_I > 0.

Requirement D.4 (Spectral controllability). The power spectral density S_{I_C}(\omega) must be specified and reproducible, so that the control condition D (Definition 5.1) can be constructed.

Requirement D.5 (Non-task-derivation). The sequence bank \mathcal{S} must satisfy P(\mathcal{S} \mid \mu) = P(\mathcal{S}) for all task distributions \mu of interest (Proposition B.7).

Requirement D.6 (Architectural constraint). The sequences must be constrained by the architecture of G_\theta, not by experience-dependent learning. Formally, if G_\theta is trained on any data, the training must not use the downstream task distribution \mu.

D.3 Candidate Implementations

Implementation D.7 (Coupled oscillators). Let I_i(t) satisfy:

\ddot{I}_i + \gamma \dot{I}_i + \omega_i^2 I_i = \sum_{j} J_{ij} I_j + \xi_i(t),

where \xi_i is colored noise, J_{ij} is a coupling matrix, and \omega_i are natural frequencies. The resulting signal has structured temporal and spatial correlations. The natural frequencies and coupling matrix are preconfigured—they are not learned from data.

Implementation D.8 (Latent dynamical system). Let h(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d follow:

h(t+1) = A h(t) + B \xi(t), \quad I(t) = C h(t),

where A has eigenvalues inside the unit circle (stability) and C projects the latent state to N observable units. The matrices A, B, C are preconfigured.

Implementation D.9 (Preconfigured sequence bank). Let \{s_k\}_{k=1}^K be a bank of preconfigured sequences s_k : [0, T_k] \to \mathbb{R}^N. At each time, the generator samples a sequence index k \sim p_k and emits I(t) = s_k(t \bmod T_k) with added small noise. The bank \mathcal{S} satisfies Requirement D.5 by construction.

Remark D.10. The three implementations differ in their degree of preconfiguration. D.7 and D.8 generate sequences dynamically from preconfigured parameters; D.9 uses a fixed bank. The choice affects what the ISA can transmit to the network. A theoretical open question is which class of implementations suffices to produce the effects predicted by H₃.

D.4 Non-Task-Derivation Verification

Procedure D.11 (Independence test). To verify Requirement D.5:

1. Generate two sequence banks \mathcal{S}_1, \mathcal{S}_2 using the same generator G_\theta but with task distributions \mu_1 \neq \mu_2.
2. Compute a distributional distance D(\hat{P}_{\mathcal{S}_1}, \hat{P}_{\mathcal{S}_2}) between the empirical distributions of the two banks.
3. Under the null hypothesis of independence, D is asymptotically distributed as a known statistic (e.g., maximum mean discrepancy with a characteristic kernel).
4. Reject the null hypothesis if D > D_{\text{crit}} at the prescribed significance level.

Remark D.12. If the null hypothesis is rejected, the generator is task-dependent and the implementation is a curriculum, not ISA. The verification is a prerequisite for any experimental claim about ISA's developmental effects.

