Appendix E — Scaffold and Maturation Specifications

E.1 Scaffold Architecture

Definition E.1 (Scaffold). The scaffold is a set of parameters Q_t \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_Q} and a set of forward functions f_Q : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^N, active only during the developmental phase [0, T_{\text{dev}}].

Property E.2 (Transience). The scaffold satisfies:

Q_t = 0 \quad \forall t > T_{\text{dev}}.

Property E.3 (Amplification). During [0, T_{\text{dev}}], the scaffold amplifies a designated subspace:

f_Q(x) = x + \alpha \Pi_Q x,

where \Pi_Q is a projection onto a low-dimensional subspace and \alpha > 0 is the amplification factor.

E.2 Maturation Schedule

Definition E.4 (Maturation operator). The maturation operator \mathcal{M} acts on (S, W, Q):

\mathcal{M} : (S_t, W_t, Q_t) \mapsto (S_{t+1}, W_{t+1}, Q_{t+1}),

with three simultaneous processes:

1. Scaffold decay:

Q_{t+1} = (1 - \rho_Q) Q_t, \quad \rho_Q \in (0, 1).

2. Plasticity decrease:

P_{t+1} = P_t \cdot \exp(-\rho_P \Delta t).

3. Structural consolidation:

W_{t+1} = W_t + \eta_t \Delta W_t, \quad \eta_t = \eta_0 \cdot \exp(-\rho_\eta t).

E.3 Critical Period Parameterization

Definition E.5 (Critical period schedule). The plasticity decay is parameterized by two scalars: the onset time t_{\text{crit}} and the decay rate \rho_P:

P(t) = P_0 \cdot \begin{cases} 1 & t < t_{\text{crit}} \\ \exp(-\rho_P (t - t_{\text{crit}})) & t \geq t_{\text{crit}} \end{cases}.

Remark E.6. Critical period research in neural language models shows that the timing of plasticity reduction (parameter t_{\text{crit}}) matters as much as its magnitude (parameter \rho_P). Weight decay applied early in training can have qualitatively different effects than weight decay applied late. The two parameters must therefore be specified independently and connected to the developmental literature. If the plasticity schedule produces effects equivalent to a generic regularization schedule, the biological interpretation of plasticity decay is unnecessary. If the effects differ, the biological specificity of the schedule is supported.

E.4 Scaffold Internalization

Proposition E.7 (Internalization condition). If S_Q \approx 1 (Definition 5.5), then the function of Q_t has been internalized into W_T. This requires that the amplification effect of Q_t be learnable by W_t during the developmental window.

Proof sketch. During [0, T_{\text{dev}}], Q_t provides an amplified signal to W_t. If the update rule for W_t is such that the amplified component is progressively encoded into W_t, then after extinction Q_t = 0, the network retains the encoding. The condition S_Q \approx 1 is the empirical verification of this internalization. \square

Remark E.8. The internalization condition is the formal bridge between scaffold-based development and scaffold-free deployment. It is also the condition that distinguishes a scaffold from a permanent architectural component: a scaffold that is not internalized is not a scaffold but a permanent crutch.

