Appendix F — Task Space and Measure Design

F.1 Structure of the Task Space

Definition F.1. The task space \mathcal{T} is a metric space (\mathcal{T}, d_{\mathcal{T}}).

Assumption F.2. \mathcal{T} admits a decomposition into families:

\mathcal{T} = \bigcup_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \mathcal{T}_f,

where each family \mathcal{T}_f has a well-defined difficulty measure.

F.2 Candidate Families

Family Description Diagnostic purpose
\mathcal{T}_{\text{IID}} Standard in-distribution tasks Baseline
\mathcal{T}_{\text{comp}} Compositional tasks Generalization
\mathcal{T}_{\text{OOD}} Out-of-distribution tasks Robustness
\mathcal{T}_{\text{transfer}} Transfer tasks Reorganization
\mathcal{T}_{\text{novel}} Tasks absent from \mathcal{T}^{\text{env}} Representability

F.3 Choice of the Measure \mu

Criterion F.3. The measure \mu on \mathcal{T} should satisfy three conditions:

1. \mu(\mathcal{T}_f) > 0 for each family f;
2. \mu should not be concentrated on tasks that are trivially easy or impossible;
3. \mu should be chosen independently of the observed results.

Remark F.4. The choice of \mu is an experimental decision that must be documented and justified a priori. A measure chosen after observing results would invalidate the statistical procedure. The recommended practice is to fix \mu by a pre-registered protocol.

Candidate F.5 (Uniform per family). \mu = \sum_f w_f \mu_f, where \mu_f is uniform on \mathcal{T}_f and w_f are predefined weights. This ensures that each family contributes proportionally to the analysis.

Candidate F.6 (Difficulty-weighted). \mu(\tau) \propto \exp(-\beta \cdot \mathrm{difficulty}(\tau)), with \beta > 0. This emphasizes moderately difficult tasks.

F.4 A Priori Validity Function

Definition F.7. For each family \mathcal{T}_f, the validity function V_t is fixed a priori by a triple (\mathrm{Synt}_f, \mathrm{Sem}_f, \mathrm{Exec}_f):

V_t(\tau) = \mathrm{Synt}_f(\tau) \cdot \mathrm{Sem}_f(\tau) \cdot \mathrm{Exec}_f(\tau)

where:

· \mathrm{Synt}_f(\tau) \in \{0, 1\} is syntactic well-formedness defined by the grammar of family f;
· \mathrm{Sem}_f(\tau) \in [0, 1] is semantic coherence defined by a fixed set of equivalence relations for family f;
· \mathrm{Exec}_f(\tau) \in [0, 1] is executability defined by a simulator or environment oracle for family f.

Remark F.8. The function V_t is not learned by the agent. It is a property of the task family, fixed before any experimental run. This eliminates the regress that would arise if V_t were learned by a process dependent on the agent's developmental state \Theta_t. The cost is domain-specificity, but this is a feature: the framework already scopes itself to specific families.

Remark F.9. The threshold v_{\min} must also be fixed a priori. Setting v_{\min} = 0 recovers the raw syntactic space \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{syn}}. Setting v_{\min} very high restricts \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}} to a subset of tasks that are both coherent and executable.

