Appendix G — Estimation of Representable and Generative Spaces

G.1 Probing Representable Space

Procedure G.1 (Representational probing).

1. Fix a candidate task set \mathcal{T}_N \subset \mathcal{U}.
2. For each \tau \in \mathcal{T}_N, train a lightweight decoder D_\tau on top of the frozen representation \phi_t.
3. Compute \mathrm{Perf}_\tau(D_\tau \circ \phi_t).
4. Declare \tau \in \widehat{\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}}} if the performance exceeds \mathrm{Perf}_{\text{chance}}(\tau) + \eta.

Proposition G.2 (Estimation bias). The estimator \widehat{\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}}} is biased upward if the decoder D_\tau has more capacity than \phi_t can support. To control bias, D_\tau should be chosen with capacity bounded in terms of the intrinsic dimensionality of \phi_t.

G.2 Sampling Generative Space

Procedure G.3 (Endogenous generation).

1. Sample a primitive task \tau_p \sim \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{prim}}.
2. Sample a sequence of operators o_1, \dots, o_k \sim \mathcal{O}_t.
3. Form the composed task \tau = o_k \circ \dots \circ o_1(\tau_p).
4. Evaluate validity V_t(\tau) (a priori, Appendix F.4).
5. If V_t(\tau) \geq v_{\min}, add \tau to \widehat{\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}}.

Definition G.4 (Generative measures).

\hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}) = |\widehat{\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}}|,

\hat{\mu}_2(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}) = \sup_{\tau \in \widehat{\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}}} \mathrm{depth}(\tau),

\hat{\mu}_3(\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}) = \frac{1}{|\widehat{\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}}|} \sum_{\tau} d(\tau, \mathcal{T}^{\text{env}}).

Caveat G.5. The three measures are complementary. A system that produces many shallow tasks has high \hat{\mu}_1 but low \hat{\mu}_2. A system that produces few deep tasks has the opposite profile. No single measure suffices.

G.3 Ordering Estimation

Procedure G.6 (Temporal ordering estimation). To estimate the ordering of geometric changes:

1. At each developmental time t_j, estimate \hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_{t_j}^{\text{rep}}), \hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_{t_j}^{\text{learn}}), \hat{\mu}_1(\mathcal{T}_{t_j}^{\text{gen}}).
2. Compute the time t_X^* at which each component X \in \{\text{rep}, \text{learn}, \text{gen}\} first exceeds its baseline by a margin \delta_X.
3. Estimate the ordering by comparing t_{\text{rep}}^*, t_{\text{learn}}^*, t_{\text{gen}}^*.

Proposition G.7. Under the null hypothesis of simultaneous geometric change, the three times t_X^* are equal in expectation. The predicted strict ordering is confirmed if t_{\text{rep}}^* < t_{\text{learn}}^* < t_{\text{gen}}^* with statistical significance under a one-sided test.

