Appendix H — Budget Equalization: Formal Accounting

H.1 Budget Components

Definition H.1. The budget vector is:

B = (B_{\text{data}}, B_{\text{compute}}, B_{\text{mem}}, B_{\text{energy}}, B_{\text{params}}).

H.2 Accounting Rules

Rule H.2 (Data budget). B_{\text{data}} counts the number of distinct environment interactions (observations, rewards, actions). ISA does not consume B_{\text{data}}.

Rule H.3 (Compute budget). B_{\text{compute}} counts floating-point operations. All operations—including ISA generation and scaffold maintenance—are accounted for.

Rule H.4 (Memory budget). B_{\text{mem}} counts peak memory usage during training, measured in bytes.

Rule H.5 (Energy budget). B_{\text{energy}} is computed as:

B_{\text{energy}} = c_{\text{FLOP}} \cdot B_{\text{compute}} + c_{\text{mem}} \cdot B_{\text{mem}},

where c_{\text{FLOP}} and c_{\text{mem}} are hardware-dependent coefficients.

Rule H.6 (Parameter budget). B_{\text{params}} counts the number of trainable parameters at the end of training, after any pruning.

H.3 Compensation Protocol

Procedure H.7 (Standard compensation). For each developmental condition C \in \{A, B, C, D\}:

1. Compute B_{\text{compute}}^{(C)} as the total compute used by the developmental trajectory.
2. Let B_{\text{compute}}^{\max} = \max_C B_{\text{compute}}^{(C)}.
3. For each C with B_{\text{compute}}^{(C)} < B_{\text{compute}}^{\max}, allocate the difference to additional training on the post-developmental task set.
4. Verify that B_{\text{data}}, B_{\text{mem}}, B_{\text{energy}}, B_{\text{params}} are equalized identically.

Procedure H.8 (Refined compensation for converged controls). If additional training on the post-developmental task set does not exhaust the compensation budget—because the control condition has converged—then:

1. Introduce random restarts of the control condition with different seeds.
2. Allocate the remaining compute to hyperparameter search over the control's learning rate, architecture width, and batch size.
3. Report the best control performance across all restarts and searches.

This gives the control condition the maximum possible advantage, making the developmental effect (if observed) conservative.

Proposition H.9. After compensation, if \mathcal{A}_C \neq \mathcal{A}_A in the sense of Definition 4.16, the difference cannot be attributed to a differential budget.

Proof. By construction, all budgets are equal after compensation. Therefore any observed difference in \mathcal{A} must arise from the structural differences in the developmental trajectories (i.e., the difference in \Theta_t). \square

H.4 Edge Cases

Remark H.10. If some developmental condition consumes compute that cannot be compensated by additional training (for example, because the training has converged and further training is unstable), then the compensation must be done at the level of the initial conditions (e.g., additional random restarts). Procedure H.8 handles this case. The residual risk is that the random restarts introduce a different kind of advantage (e.g., better initialization) that is not attributable to development. This risk must be documented and, where possible, controlled by matching the number of restarts across conditions.

Remark H.11. The budget equalization does not equalize wall-clock time. A developmental trajectory may be longer in wall-clock time than a non-developmental one, even if the FLOP budget is equal. This is a genuine limitation: if wall-clock time is a resource (as it is in biological development), then equalizing FLOPs does not equalize the developmental opportunity. The framework should specify whether wall-clock time is part of the budget vector or not. If it is, the budget vector gains a sixth component B_{\text{time}}.

