Appendix J — Glossary of Terms

Agent. A system characterized by a state X_t, an update rule, and an interaction protocol with an environment.

Budget. A vector B of five components controlling the resources available to an agent.

Curriculum. An operator on the data distribution that does not modify the structural degrees of freedom \Theta_t.

Developmental trajectory. A sequence \{X_t\}_{t=0}^{T} of states generated by the dynamics F under a developmental protocol.

Development. An operator that modifies the structural degrees of freedom \Theta_t of the learner.

Developmental Transfer Operator. A functional \mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{R}} that maps an initial learning cost functional \mathcal{A}_t to a final one \mathcal{A}_{t+\Delta}, depending on the trajectory \mathcal{R}.

Developmental distance. A measure d_{\mathcal{A}} of the difference between two agents in terms of their learning cost functionals.

Developmental Task Geometry. The tuple \mathcal{G}_t = (\mathcal{T}_t^{\text{rep}}, \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{learn}}, \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}}, \mathcal{O}_t, \mathcal{A}_t).

Endogenous plasticity. A plasticity variable P_t whose evolution depends on the state of the agent, not only on an external schedule.

Intrinsic Structured Activity (ISA). Endogenous activity with spatiotemporal structure, coupled to circuit maturation, and independent of the downstream task distribution.

Learning cost. The minimal number of updates required to reach a performance threshold 1 - \epsilon with probability 1 - \delta under budget B.

Learning space. The set \mathcal{L}_t of tasks whose learning cost is below a threshold T_{\max}.

Scaffold. A transient set of parameters or functions active only during the developmental phase.

Structural degrees of freedom. The tuple \Theta_t = (C_t, P_t, Q_t, \mathcal{R}_t^{\text{learn}}) representing the metaplastic and structural properties of the learner.

Valid generative space. The set \mathcal{T}_t^{\text{gen}} of tasks that are both syntactically generable and valid.

Volterra expansion. A functional expansion of \Psi_\Delta in the trajectory, with kernels K_1, K_2, \ldots capturing linear, quadratic, and higher-order contributions.

