Appendix K — Open Problems and Theoretical Extensions

K.1 Continuous Composition Operators

Problem K.1. The formalization assumes \mathcal{O}_t is a discrete set. Extend to \mathcal{O}_t \subseteq C^\infty(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{T}) with a metric.

Approach K.2. Define a metric on \mathcal{O}_t via the operator norm:

d_{\mathcal{O}}(o_1, o_2) = \sup_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}} \frac{d_{\mathcal{T}}(o_1(\tau), o_2(\tau))}{\|\tau\|_{\mathcal{T}} + 1}.

This permits a notion of continuous deformation of the generative space.

K.2 Trajectory Optimization

Problem K.3. Given a target task set \mathcal{T}^* and a budget B, find the developmental trajectory \mathcal{R}^* that maximizes \mathcal{A}_T restricted to \mathcal{T}^*:

\mathcal{R}^* = \arg\min_{\mathcal{R}} \int_{\mathcal{T}^*} \mathcal{A}_T^{(\mathcal{R})}(\tau) d\mu(\tau).

Approach K.4. Formulate as an optimal control problem with state X_t, control (I_t, Q_t, P_t), and cost functional depending on \mathcal{A}_T. The Volterra expansion (4.13) provides a tractable approximation: truncate at second order and optimize the kernels.

K.3 Coupling Trajectory

Problem K.5. Determine the optimal coupling trajectory \lambda(t) \in [0, 1] between intrinsic and extrinsic activity.

Hypothesis K.6. The optimal \lambda(t) is sigmoidal, with a slow initial increase (predominance of ISA) and a rapid transition to extrinsic dominance.

Approach K.7. Treat \lambda(t) as a control variable and solve a two-point boundary value problem with the constraint that the final state X_T belongs to a target set.

K.4 Theory of Reasoning as Trajectory Property

Problem K.8. Formulate the conditions under which a modification of \mathcal{G}_T constitutes reasoning.

Candidate conditions K.9.

1. Compositional closure: If \tau_1, \tau_2 \in \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen}}, then their composition is also in \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen}}.
2. Truth preservation: If \tau_1 and \tau_2 are valid, then their composition is valid.
3. Systematic transfer: If \tau is learnable, then its compositional variants are also learnable with sublinear cost growth.
4. Metacognitive access: The system can evaluate its own competence on generated tasks.

These conditions are formal and testable. They distinguish the generative capacity of H₄₊ from the reasoning capacity of H_{\text{reasoning}}.

K.5 Extension to Multi-Agent Development

Problem K.10. Extend the framework to multiple agents with shared environment and partial observation.

Approach K.11. Define the joint state X_t^{(1:N)} and the joint operator \mathcal{D}^{(1:N)}. The developmental distance becomes a matrix d_{\mathcal{A}}^{(i,j)}.

K.6 Relationship to Predictive Coding and Free Energy

Problem K.12. Relate the ISA framework to predictive coding and the free energy principle.

Conjecture K.13. ISA may be interpretable as an intrinsic prior that shapes the generative model before external data arrive. The developmental trajectory would then correspond to a schedule of prior relaxation.

This conjecture is testable: if true, agents with ISA should exhibit lower variational free energy on novel tasks after development, compared to matched controls.

K.7 Critical Period Reverse-Engineering

Problem K.14. Determine whether the plasticity schedule P(t) produces effects distinguishable from generic regularization schedules.

Approach K.15. Compare the developmental condition (with P(t) schedule) to a control condition with a weight-decay schedule matched for total regularization magnitude. If the effects are statistically indistinguishable, the biological interpretation of plasticity decay is unnecessary. If they differ, the biological specificity is supported.

