Appendix N — Measurement Protocols for Reasoning Conditions

N.1 Compositional Closure

Procedure N.1. To test compositional closure (Condition K.9.1):

1. Sample \tau_1, \tau_2 \in \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen}} independently.
2. Form \tau = \tau_2 \circ \tau_1 using the composition operators \mathcal{O}_T.
3. Evaluate V_T(\tau) using the a priori validity function (Appendix F.4).
4. Declare closure satisfied if V_T(\tau) \geq v_{\min} for a fraction 1 - \epsilon_{\text{closure}} of samples, where \epsilon_{\text{closure}} is a pre-registered tolerance.

N.2 Truth Preservation

Procedure N.2. To test truth preservation (Condition K.9.2):

1. Fix a set of atomic valid tasks \mathcal{T}_{\text{atom}} \subset \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen}}.
2. For each composition \tau = o_k \circ \dots \circ o_1(\tau_p), verify that the validity of \tau implies the validity of each component and vice versa.
3. Truth preservation is satisfied if the equivalence holds for a fraction 1 - \epsilon_{\text{truth}} of compositions.

Remark N.3. This procedure requires a formal notion of truth for the task family. For propositional tasks, truth is classical satisfaction; for probabilistic tasks, truth is high-probability satisfaction; for procedural tasks, truth is successful execution.

N.3 Systematic Transfer

Procedure N.3. To test systematic transfer (Condition K.9.3):

1. Train the agent on a task \tau.
2. Evaluate on a composition \tau' = o(\tau) with o \in \mathcal{O}_T.
3. Measure the transfer cost T_{1 \to 2}(\tau, \tau') = \mathcal{A}_T(\tau') - \mathcal{A}_T(\tau).
4. Systematic transfer is satisfied if T_{1 \to 2} grows sublinearly in the depth of the composition: T_{1 \to 2}(\tau, o^k(\tau)) = \mathcal{O}(k^\alpha) with \alpha < 1.

N.4 Metacognitive Access

Procedure N.4. To test metacognitive access (Condition K.9.4):

1. Sample a task \tau \in \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen}}.
2. Ask the agent to predict its own performance \hat{P}_\tau before attempting \tau.
3. Measure the calibration error |\hat{P}_\tau - P_\tau|.
4. Metacognitive access is satisfied if the calibration error is below a pre-registered threshold \epsilon_{\text{meta}} for a fraction 1 - \epsilon_{\text{cal}} of tasks.

Remark N.5. Metacognitive access is the strongest of the four conditions. It requires the agent to have a model of its own competence, which is a higher-order capacity. It is included for completeness but is not required for H₄₊.

N.5 Relation Between Conditions

Proposition N.6. The four conditions form a hierarchy:

\text{Metacognitive access} \Rightarrow \text{Systematic transfer} \Rightarrow \text{Truth preservation} \Rightarrow \text{Compositional closure}.

Proof. Metacognitive access requires a model of competence, which requires the ability to evaluate transfer (systematic transfer). Systematic transfer requires the ability to preserve validity under composition (truth preservation), which requires closure. The implications are strict: each condition is necessary but not sufficient for the next. \square

Remark N.7. The hierarchy N.6 clarifies the relationship between H₄₊ and H_{\text{reasoning}}. H₄₊ requires only that \mathcal{T}_T^{\text{gen}} differ between trajectories. H_{\text{reasoning}} requires that the differences satisfy the four conditions. The gap between the two is the space of possible findings: a system may have a larger generative space (H₄₊) without the space being closed under composition (Condition N.1), let alone truth-preserving (Condition N.2). The measurement protocols N.1–N.4 are the instruments for determining where in the hierarchy a given system lies.

